import type { ResearchPost } from './types';

export const november06ResearchPosts: readonly ResearchPost[] = [{"slug":"how-much-research-time-does-a-qualified-account-require","title":"How much research time does a qualified account require?","summary":"A time-study protocol for measuring the preparation behind one qualified account, with categories, sampling, and honest limits.","cluster":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"Research capacity becomes manageable when the team measures the work by account type, records rework, and reports a median with a range instead of a single average.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/how-much-research-time-does-a-qualified-account-require.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks a narrow operational question: how much staff time does it take to prepare one qualified account for a first conversation? The unit is a single account that has passed the team's fit check and is waiting for a BDR. The measure is active research and documentation time, not the elapsed time between request and completion. The goal is a repeatable estimate the team can use for capacity planning. The estimate should be reported for the team's current process, not for an idealized queue.","The question matters because research capacity is usually described in headcount rather than hours. A team may believe it has one full-time researcher when the actual work is spread across calls, CRM updates, and last-minute preparation. Without a time estimate, it is hard to decide whether to add support, reduce the research standard, or change the queue. The estimate also gives the owner a way to compare the cost of preparation with the value of the conversation. A short definition of active time keeps the number comparable across researchers."]},{"heading":"Why the question matters","paragraphs":["A qualified account is not a free input. Someone has to read the source, check the fit, write the brief, and update the record. When that work is invisible, it competes with work that is easier to measure, such as calls and emails. The result is often a queue that looks healthy while the preparation is rushed or skipped. The cost of preparation is easiest to see when it is recorded as a separate task.","The estimate should not be used to pressure the researcher into faster work. It should be used to make the work visible and to decide how many accounts the team can prepare well. A team that knows the real time can set a realistic service level. A team that guesses will either overload the researcher or underuse the queue. A service level built on guesswork usually breaks during the first busy week."]},{"heading":"Unit of analysis","paragraphs":["Define the unit before collecting any timings. A new account with no prior research is different from an account that was reviewed last quarter. A warm introduction is different from a cold list import. Record the account type, the source of the request, and whether any research already exists. These distinctions explain most of the variation in time. A warm account with recent notes should be timed separately from a cold account.","Decide whether revisions count. If a brief is returned for missing evidence, the rework belongs in the total because it is part of the real cost. If the owner asks for a second brief after a call, that is a new unit unless the team decides to treat it as a revision. The rule should be written down before the first timing session. The rework rule should be written before the first returned brief is counted."]},{"heading":"Time categories","paragraphs":["Break the work into categories that the researcher can time without stopping every few minutes. The categories might be record review, source search, evidence check, brief writing, CRM entry, and review. Keep the list short enough to use on a simple timer. A category called other is useful but should not become the largest one. The categories should match the steps in the written research checklist.","Record interruptions separately. A researcher who answers a call in the middle of a brief has not spent that time on the account. If interruptions are common, the team may need a protected block for research or a different queue design. The time study should show the cost of the current design, not an idealized version of it. Interruptions should be noted even when they last only a few minutes."]},{"heading":"Collection method","paragraphs":["Use a small sample of accounts and time them over a normal week. A sample that includes only the easiest accounts will understate the work. Include new accounts, returning accounts, and at least one account with a difficult source. The researcher should start and stop the timer at the same points for every case. The sample should include at least one account that required a second source check.","Keep a short note for each timing session: the account type, the categories used, and anything unusual. The note helps explain outliers later. It also gives the reviewer a way to check whether the timer was used consistently. A timer without context is just a number. The note should say whether the timer ran during a meeting or a call."]},{"heading":"Calculation","paragraphs":["Report the median and the range, not only the average. A few very long research tasks can pull the average up and make the typical case look worse than it is. The median shows the middle of the distribution, while the range shows how much the work varies. Both are useful for planning. The median is more stable than the average when one account takes much longer.","Calculate the total for each account by adding the categories and any rework. Then group the totals by account type and source. If one group is much slower, examine the source or the brief rather than assuming the researcher is slower. The calculation should point to a cause the team can act on. The raw totals should be kept so a reviewer can check the grouping."]},{"heading":"Reading the pattern","paragraphs":["A pattern is not a performance score. A long research time may mean the source is hard to verify, the brief asks for too much, or the account is genuinely complex. A short time may mean the researcher skipped a step or the account had recent work. Read the numbers with the notes and a sample of the briefs. The notes should be read before the numbers are used to change the queue.","Look for categories that dominate the total. If source search is the largest category, the team may need a better approved-source list. If brief writing dominates, the template may be too long. If CRM entry dominates, the fields may be poorly designed. The time study should lead to a design change, not a blame conversation. A design change should be tested on the next sample before it becomes policy."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The estimate describes the team's current process, not an industry benchmark. Different offers, regions, and account sizes will produce different times. The sample may miss busy periods or unusual accounts. The researcher may also change their pace because they know they are being timed. The report should state the sample size and the date range next to the estimate.","Do not use the estimate to promise a fixed turnaround without accounting for queue depth and review capacity. Research time is only one part of the cycle. The owner's review, the BDR's availability, and the account's response time also shape the result. State those limits when the estimate is shared. The estimate should separate research time from review and queue time."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the estimate with the method, the sample size, and the date. Include the categories, the median, and the range. Explain what was excluded and why. A short method note makes the number usable by someone who was not in the room. One worked example helps a reader follow the calculation without the raw file.","Use the estimate to set a realistic number of accounts per researcher per week. Then check the estimate against actual output after a month. If the two diverge, review the queue and the standard before changing the headcount. The point is a better operating decision, not a precise number. A monthly comparison is more useful than a single estimate from one week."]},{"heading":"From estimate to staffing decision","paragraphs":["The estimate becomes useful when it is translated into a staffing decision the owner can defend. Start with the number of qualified accounts the team expects in a normal week, then multiply by the median research time for the relevant account type. Add the review time and a small allowance for rework, because both are part of the real cycle. Compare that total with the hours actually available before deciding whether to add capacity or narrow the queue.","Do not use the estimate to promise a turnaround that depends on an owner who is also in meetings all week. The research time and the review time are separate constraints, and the slower one sets the pace. If the owner can review only ten briefs in a week, preparing twenty briefs does not increase throughput. The staffing decision should protect the review step as carefully as the research step."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Census Business Builder","url":"https://www.census.gov/data/data-tools/cbb.html"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"}]},{"slug":"what-makes-a-bdr-handoff-note-reliable-under-review","title":"What makes a BDR handoff note reliable under review?","summary":"A review protocol for handoff notes, built around five fields a second reviewer can verify without asking the original author.","cluster":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"A handoff note is reliable when a reviewer can reconstruct the state, the evidence, the owner, and the next date from the record alone, without a conversation.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/what-makes-a-bdr-handoff-note-reliable-under-review.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what makes a BDR handoff note reliable when someone else reviews it. Reliability means a second person can understand the account state, the evidence behind it, the current owner, and the next date without asking the original author. The unit is one handoff event between a researcher, a BDR, and an account owner. The review concerns the written record, not the quality of the conversation that produced it. The review should state which records were excluded and why.","The scope excludes sales forecasts, performance ratings, and legal conclusions. A note can be reliable and still describe a weak opportunity. The purpose is to separate record quality from opportunity quality so the team can improve the handoff without turning the review into a judgment of the person who wrote it. The review should record the sample frame before reading any notes."]},{"heading":"Why handoffs fail","paragraphs":["Handoffs fail when the note assumes context that only the author holds. A line such as waiting on legal or interested but timing unclear feels informative until someone else has to act on it. The reviewer cannot tell what was verified, who said it, or what should happen next. The result is a second research cycle that costs more than the original handoff. A note that lists activity without a conclusion forces the next person to repeat the work.","Another common failure is a note that records activity instead of state. A list of calls and emails shows effort but not the current position. The reviewer needs the conclusion and the evidence, not a chronological log. A short structured note is usually more reliable than a long narrative because it forces the author to name the missing pieces. The template should prompt for the missing piece instead of rewarding a longer note."]},{"heading":"Unit and review sample","paragraphs":["Define the handoff event as the moment a record moves from research to active BDR work. Include the note, the linked evidence, and the assignment. Sample handoffs across account types and owners rather than only the ones that felt difficult. A random sample is more informative than a review of the cases everyone already remembers. The sample should include handoffs from different account tiers and owners.","Decide how many handoffs to review before looking at the results. A small sample reviewed consistently is better than a large sample reviewed once. Record the sample frame, the date range, and the selection rule. If the team changes the note template during the review, start a new sample so the comparison stays honest. A fixed sample makes the next review comparable even when the queue changes."]},{"heading":"The five fields","paragraphs":["A reliable note usually contains five fields: the current state, the supporting evidence, the open question, the owner, and the next date. The current state says what is true now. The evidence says how the author knows. The open question says what remains unresolved. The owner and date make the next step actionable. The five fields should fit on one screen without scrolling.","Each field should have a written definition and an example. The state should avoid adjectives that cannot be verified. The evidence should link to a source or name the conversation and date. The open question should be specific enough to answer. The owner should be a person, not a team, and the date should be a calendar date. The owner field should name a person, not a team or an inbox."]},{"heading":"Evidence standards","paragraphs":["Not every claim needs a public source. A buyer's stated priority may come from a call, and that is acceptable when the note says who said it and when. A claim about company size or leadership should link to a public source if one exists. The standard depends on the claim, but the note should always show the basis. A conversation note should name the speaker and the date.","Separate what was observed from what was inferred. A note might observe that a new office opened and infer that local support may be needed. The observation can be verified, while the inference should be labeled as a hypothesis. That distinction helps the next reviewer decide which part to test in the first conversation. The inference should be labeled so the next reviewer does not repeat it as fact."]},{"heading":"Review procedure","paragraphs":["Ask a reviewer who did not write the note to reconstruct the account state from the record alone. Give the reviewer a short form with the five fields and a place to mark what is missing. Do not let the author explain the note during the reconstruction. The exercise tests the record, not the author's memory. The reviewer should mark where the record was unclear, not fill the gap from memory.","After the reconstruction, compare the reviewer's understanding with the author's intent. Every difference points to a field that needs a clearer definition or a missing piece of evidence. Record the differences by type so the team can see whether the problem is structure, evidence, or ownership. Fix the template before coaching individual writers. The comparison should be done before the author explains the note."]},{"heading":"Scoring and interpretation","paragraphs":["A simple score can make the review repeatable: one point for each field that is present and verifiable, with a note for any field that is present but vague. Do not collapse the score into a single pass or fail without keeping the field-level detail. The detail is what tells the team which part of the handoff to repair. The score sheet should stay with the note so the next review uses the same fields.","Interpret low scores by field rather than by person. If the open question is often missing, the template may not prompt for it. If the owner is often a team name, the assignment step may be too loose. If the evidence is often absent, the research task may not include a source requirement. The pattern should lead to a process change. A score without the field detail is hard to turn into a training plan."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The review measures the written record, not the relationship. A strong note can sit on a weak opportunity, and a weak note can describe a strong one. The reviewer's own experience may shape what counts as clear. The sample may miss handoffs that never reached the review because the record was never created. The report should state the sample size and the review date.","Do not use the score as a performance rating without context. A new BDR may write thinner notes because the template is unfamiliar, while an experienced BDR may omit fields out of habit. The purpose is to improve the shared record. Pair the score with training and template changes rather than individual warnings. A short calibration session before the review reduces variation between reviewers."]},{"heading":"Using the result","paragraphs":["Report the field-level results with the sample size and the date. Show which fields improved after a template change and which ones stayed weak. Keep the report short enough for the team to read in a meeting. A clear pattern is more useful than a long list of individual notes. The report should use the same field names as the review form.","Repeat the review after the template changes and compare the same fields. If the score does not move, the problem may be training or incentives rather than structure. If it moves but the BDRs still ask for context, the note may be complete without being useful. The final test is whether the next person can act without a meeting. A second review after the template change shows whether the fix worked."]},{"heading":"Turning the review into a training loop","paragraphs":["The review is most useful when its findings become a short training loop rather than an annual audit. Pick the field that failed most often in the sample and write one example that shows the difference between a vague entry and a complete one. Use that example in the next team session and in the template prompt. Then review a new sample after two weeks to see whether the field improved.","Keep the loop small enough to repeat. A single field, one example, and a two-week check are easier to sustain than a full redesign of the handoff process. If the field improves, move to the next weakest link. If it does not, ask whether the field is hard to use or the team does not see why it matters. The answer may be a tool change rather than more training."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"}]},{"slug":"where-do-follow-ups-stall-in-a-shared-bdr-queue","title":"Where do follow-ups stall in a shared BDR queue?","summary":"A stage-timestamp method for finding the handoff where follow-ups lose time, with business-hour rules and clear limits.","cluster":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"Most queue delay becomes visible only when each stage has a timestamp, an owner, and a rule for whether waiting time counts in business hours or calendar days.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/where-do-follow-ups-stall-in-a-shared-bdr-queue.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks where follow-ups lose time in a shared BDR queue. The unit is one follow-up task from the moment it enters the queue to the moment it is sent, closed, or escalated. The measure is the time spent in each stage, not the total age of the account. The goal is to find the handoff that creates the most delay so the team can repair that step. The method note should state which systems supplied the timestamps.","The scope includes tasks owned by researchers, BDRs, and account owners. It excludes the buyer's response time, which the team does not control. Separating internal delay from external waiting is the first requirement for a useful analysis. Without that separation, a slow queue and a slow buyer look the same in the report. Internal delay and buyer delay should be reported separately."]},{"heading":"Why shared queues hide delay","paragraphs":["A shared queue makes work visible but not necessarily accountable. A task can sit in the same status while three people assume someone else is handling it. The queue looks stable because nothing changes, and stability is mistaken for progress. Stage timestamps expose the difference between waiting and working. A queue that never shows who is waiting invites a second follow-up.","Shared ownership also makes it easy to reset a date without changing the task. A follow-up can be pushed forward repeatedly while the account remains in the same position. The analysis should count reschedules as a signal, not as normal maintenance. A task that moves three times without a new fact is a candidate for review. A timestamp is useful only when the status change is recorded at the moment it happens."]},{"heading":"Definitions and unit","paragraphs":["Define the queue stages before extracting any data. A simple model is intake, research, review, first touch, follow-up, and closed. Each stage needs a start event and an end event that the system records automatically. If a stage is defined by a person's memory, the timing will not be comparable across tasks. The stage model should match the labels the team actually uses in the queue.","Decide whether a reopened task is a new unit or a continuation. A reopened task that keeps the same evidence may belong to the original cycle, while a task that returns with a new request may be a new unit. Write the rule down and apply it to every task in the sample. The choice changes the denominator, so it should be visible in the report. The reopened-task rule should be applied before the sample is drawn."]},{"heading":"Data sources","paragraphs":["Use the system that records the task status, not a separate spreadsheet maintained after the fact. The CRM, the outreach tool, and the task manager may each hold part of the history. Compare the timestamps before trusting any one source. If the systems disagree, record the disagreement as part of the finding. The export should include the owner, the status, and the event time.","Capture the owner at each stage, the status change, and the timestamp. Add the priority, the account tier, and the source of the task. These attributes help explain why one stage is slower for some work. Without them, the analysis can only show an average, which is rarely actionable. A manual lookup should not replace a missing system timestamp."]},{"heading":"Business hours and calendar days","paragraphs":["Choose one clock and use it consistently. Business-hour delay excludes nights and weekends, while calendar-day delay includes them. A queue that operates across time zones needs a written rule for whose business hours apply. The rule should match how the team actually staffs the queue. The clock rule should be written down before the first task is timed.","Report both measures when the difference matters. A task that waits over a weekend may look slow in calendar days and normal in business hours. A task that waits during a working day looks slow in both. Showing both prevents a misleading conclusion about the team's responsiveness. A single measure can hide a weekend effect that changes the conclusion."]},{"heading":"Stage aging calculation","paragraphs":["Calculate the time between the start and end timestamp for each stage, then subtract any period when the task was intentionally paused by the buyer. Keep the pause reason and duration in the record. A pause without a reason should count as queue time, not as buyer time. The pause note should name the person who approved the pause.","Report the median and the upper range for each stage. The median shows the typical experience, while the upper range shows the tasks that may need a different process. Count the number of tasks that exceeded the team's service level. That count is often more useful than a single average. The upper range should be shown with the count of tasks it represents."]},{"heading":"Reading the pattern","paragraphs":["Look for the stage with the largest share of total delay. If research is the slowest stage, the source list or the brief may be too demanding. If review is slowest, the approver may be unavailable or the packet may be incomplete. If first touch is slowest, the assignment or the capacity may be the problem. Each pattern points to a different repair. Name that stage in the report so the repair has a clear target.","Check whether the delay is concentrated in a few tasks or spread across the queue. A small number of very old tasks can distort the average, while a broad shift suggests a systemic issue. Segment the results by owner, account tier, and task source. A pattern that appears only in one segment is easier to fix than a queue-wide change. The segment view should use the same definitions as the stage table."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The analysis describes the queue in one period and may not predict a busy month. The timestamps depend on people updating status, and some updates may be late or missing. The definition of a pause can be applied inconsistently. The team should review a sample of records to check that the timestamps match the real work. Include the start and end dates of the observation window.","Do not use the results to rank individuals without accounting for assignment and complexity. A BDR who receives the hardest accounts may show longer stage times for reasons outside their control. Use the analysis to improve routing and capacity, then review individual performance with the context of the queue. A short note on assignment difficulty makes the comparison fairer."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the stage medians, the service-level counts, and the sample size. Add a short note on the clock, the pause rule, and any missing data. Show the result by stage before showing it by owner. The stage view is usually the most actionable because it points to a handoff the team can redesign. The table should be readable without a chart.","Use the findings to change one part of the queue at a time. A new assignment rule, a shorter review packet, or a protected research block can each be tested. Measure the same stages after the change and compare the results. The goal is a queue that moves with fewer invisible waits, not a perfect dashboard. A follow-up measurement after the change shows whether the queue improved."]},{"heading":"From stage timing to a queue repair","paragraphs":["A stage timing table becomes actionable only when the team agrees on one repair and one success measure. Choose the stage with the largest share of delay and write a specific change, such as a shorter review packet or a protected research block. Define what should improve, whether that is the median stage time or the number of tasks past the service window. Then measure the same stages after the change.","Do not change three parts of the queue at once. A new assignment rule, a new template, and a new service window together make it impossible to tell which one helped. Start with the stage that affects the most tasks, keep the rest stable for a month, and compare the result. If the change does not move the measure, revert it before adding another experiment."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"}]},{"slug":"how-should-a-growth-team-measure-outreach-personalization-quality","title":"How should a growth team measure outreach personalization quality?","summary":"A rubric-based method for reviewing personalization quality with calibrated reviewers, defined dimensions, and honest limits.","cluster":"Partnership systems","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"Personalization quality is measurable when reviewers score the evidence, relevance, specificity, tone, and next step separately, then check whether two reviewers agree.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/how-should-a-growth-team-measure-outreach-personalization-quality.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how a growth team can measure the quality of personalization in outreach without reducing it to a reply rate. The unit is one sent message and the evidence used to write it. The measure is a rubric score across five dimensions, reviewed by someone other than the author. The scope excludes deliverability, list size, and revenue attribution. The rubric should score the message and the evidence packet together.","The question matters because personalization can be both expensive and shallow. A message that inserts a company name and a recent headline may look personalized while adding little to the conversation. A better measure asks whether the message uses evidence that is relevant to the offer and specific enough to deserve a reply. That is a quality question, not a volume question. A quality review explains why an outcome changed; it does not replace the outcome measure."]},{"heading":"Why reply rate is not enough","paragraphs":["Reply rate depends on the list, the offer, the timing, and the sender as well as the message. A high reply rate can come from a warm list with weak personalization, while a strong message to a cold list may receive few replies. Using reply rate alone makes it hard to tell whether the writing improved or the conditions changed. A quality review should compare messages that faced similar conditions.","A quality review does not replace outcome measurement. It explains why an outcome may have changed and gives the writer specific feedback. The two measures should be read together. A message can be well personalized and still fail because the offer is wrong, and that is a different problem from a message that never used the evidence. The review should compare messages within the same sequence and audience."]},{"heading":"Unit and sample","paragraphs":["Define the unit as a single message plus the evidence packet used to produce it. Include the account context, the source links, and the draft. Sample messages across writers, sequences, and account tiers. A sample drawn only from the best-performing sequence will not show the range of quality. The evidence packet should be the one the writer actually used.","Choose the sample size before reviewing and keep it stable across rounds. A small sample reviewed consistently is more useful than a large sample reviewed once. Record the date range and the selection rule. If the team changes the sequence during the review, start a new sample so the comparison is fair. A fixed sample makes it easier to compare results across quarters."]},{"heading":"Rubric dimensions","paragraphs":["Score evidence first. The message should use at least one verifiable detail that is relevant to the offer, not a generic fact that could apply to any company. Score relevance next. The detail should connect to a plausible need or trigger. These two dimensions carry the most weight because they determine whether the personalization is real. The score should make the reviewer name the evidence before judging the writing.","Then score specificity, tone, and next step. Specificity asks whether the message names a concrete situation instead of a broad claim. Tone asks whether it sounds like a person who understands the context. Next step asks whether the request is clear and easy to answer. Each dimension needs a short definition and an example at the low, middle, and high end. Each level of the scale should be described in one sentence."]},{"heading":"Reviewer calibration","paragraphs":["Have two reviewers score the same set before scoring the full sample. Compare their scores and discuss every difference. If the difference comes from a vague definition, rewrite the rubric. If it comes from a disagreement about the account, record the context and move on. The goal is a shared standard, not perfect agreement. The discussion should focus on the definition, not on who is right.","Calculate a simple agreement measure and report it with the results. A rubric with low agreement produces numbers that look precise but mean little. Calibration should be repeated when the offer, the sequence, or the reviewer group changes. A short calibration session is cheaper than a quarter of misleading scores. The agreement score should be reported next to the quality score."]},{"heading":"Scoring and aggregation","paragraphs":["Use a small scale, such as zero to two for each dimension, and publish the definition for each value. Sum the scores but also report the dimension-level results. The total is useful for a quick view, while the dimensions show where the writing is weak. Do not hide the dimension detail behind a single average. The dimension scores should point to a specific next action.","Aggregate by sequence, writer, and account tier, but be careful with small groups. A writer with three messages may look better or worse than a writer with thirty for reasons unrelated to skill. Report the count with every average and avoid ranking people on a small sample. The purpose is coaching and process improvement. A small group result should be labeled as directional rather than definitive."]},{"heading":"Reading the results","paragraphs":["A low evidence score suggests the research packet is thin or the writer is not using it. A low relevance score suggests the evidence is accurate but not connected to the offer. A low tone score may point to a template that sounds like marketing copy. A low next-step score may mean the call to action is vague or asks for too much. Each weak dimension should have a named owner before the next review.","Look for patterns across sequences before judging individual messages. If one sequence consistently scores low on relevance, the problem may be the offer or the audience rather than the writer. If one writer scores low on evidence but high on tone, the fix is research support rather than a writing class. Match the repair to the dimension. A pattern across sequences is more useful than a single weak message."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The rubric measures the quality of the written message, not the strength of the relationship. A well-written message can be sent to the wrong person. The reviewers may share a bias toward a certain style, and the sample may miss messages that were never sent because they failed an approval gate. State these limits when the results are shared. The report should state the sample size and the date range.","Do not use the score as the only input for compensation or promotion. Personalization quality is one part of a larger job that includes account selection, timing, and follow-up. Use the review to improve the system and to give specific feedback. A score without context can discourage the behavior the team wants to build. The score should be used to target coaching, not to rank people."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the sample size, the date range, the dimension averages, and the agreement measure. Include one or two anonymized examples at the high and low end. The examples make the rubric concrete and help the team see what a better message looks like. Keep the report short enough to discuss in a working session. The examples should be anonymized so the discussion stays on the writing.","Use the findings to change one part of the system at a time. Improve the evidence packet, rewrite the rubric, or add a review step for the weakest dimension. Then rescore a new sample and compare. The goal is a measurable improvement in the quality of the conversation, not a perfect score on a dashboard. One system change at a time makes it easier to see what improved."]},{"heading":"Connecting quality to the next test","paragraphs":["A quality score is useful only when it changes the next message or the next research packet. Pick the weakest dimension from the review and write one concrete change for the next sequence. If evidence is weak, add a required source field to the brief. If relevance is weak, rewrite the prompt that connects the evidence to the offer. Keep the change narrow so the next review can show whether it worked.","Do not use the score to declare a winner after one round. A small sample can move for reasons unrelated to the writing, and a single strong message can lift an average. Compare the same dimension across two rounds with a stable sample. The goal is a trend the team can trust, not a number that looks precise on a slide."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Census Business Builder","url":"https://www.census.gov/data/data-tools/cbb.html"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"what-belongs-in-a-partnership-research-source-log","title":"What belongs in a partnership research source log?","summary":"A field guide for a partnership source log that keeps claims, dates, access notes, and review windows usable.","cluster":"Partnership systems","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"A partnership source log earns trust when every entry names the claim it supports, the access date, the source type, and the next review date.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/what-belongs-in-a-partnership-research-source-log.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what a partnership research source log should contain so the team can use it during a live conversation and review it later. The unit is one source entry linked to one claim about a partner or market. The scope includes public sources, internal documents, and conversation notes, but excludes personal data that the team does not need for the decision. A log that cannot answer those questions is only a folder of links.","The log is not a reading list. It is a working record of the evidence behind a partnership decision. A good entry lets a reviewer find the source, understand what it supports, and judge whether it is still current. That standard is more demanding than saving a link, and it is what makes the log useful when the original researcher is unavailable. The scope should exclude personal data that the team does not need for the decision."]},{"heading":"Why a source log matters","paragraphs":["Partnership conversations often move quickly, and the evidence behind a claim can disappear into a slide or an email. When a new stakeholder asks why a partner was selected, the team needs more than a summary. The log preserves the link between the claim and the source so the answer does not depend on memory. The log should show which questions still have no source.","A log also reduces repeated research. A new team member can see which sources were already checked and which questions remain open. That saves time and prevents the team from citing an outdated page that someone else already flagged. The log becomes a shared asset rather than a personal notebook. A short index by partner makes the log usable during a live call."]},{"heading":"The source record","paragraphs":["Each entry should be tied to one claim, not to a whole document. A single report may support several claims, and each claim should have its own row with the relevant page or section. This makes the log easier to search and prevents a broad citation from standing in for a specific fact. The row should include the source title, the publisher, and the URL or file location. One entry should support one claim, not an entire report.","Add the source type, such as public filing, company page, news article, industry report, internal document, or conversation. The type tells the reviewer how much weight to give the evidence. A conversation note and a regulatory filing are both useful, but they are not interchangeable. The log should make that difference visible at a glance. The source type should be stored as a controlled value, not free text."]},{"heading":"Required fields","paragraphs":["A practical log includes the claim, the source, the access date, the source type, the confidence, the owner, and the next review date. The claim should be written as a short statement, not a topic. The access date tells the reader when the source was checked. The owner is the person who can answer questions about the entry. The claim field should stay short enough to scan in a table.","The confidence field should use a small controlled list, such as confirmed, likely, or unverified. Define each value with an example. The next review date should reflect how quickly the source type changes. A leadership page may need a shorter review window than a company registration. The dates make the log a living document instead of a static archive. The review date should be visible in the saved view the researcher uses."]},{"heading":"Evidence standards","paragraphs":["Prefer primary sources when they are available. A company announcement is stronger than a summary of the announcement, and a public filing is stronger than a news report about the filing. When only a secondary source exists, record what it summarizes and why the primary source was unavailable. That note helps the next reviewer decide whether to dig further. A primary source is stronger than a summary of that source.","Do not copy long passages into the log. A short quotation is useful when the exact wording matters, but most entries need a paraphrase and a link. If the source is sensitive, record only the minimum needed for the decision and follow the team's access rules. The log should be useful without becoming a store of unnecessary personal data. A sensitive source should be summarized with the minimum detail needed."]},{"heading":"Access and retention","paragraphs":["Decide who can read and edit the log before it fills up. Partnership research may include commercial terms, personal contact details, or internal assessments that should not be shared broadly. Use the team's existing access groups rather than creating a new sharing model. Review the permissions when a partner or team member changes. The log should not become a second permission system.","Set a retention rule for entries that are no longer active. Keep the evidence needed for an audit or a renewal, and remove personal details that are no longer necessary. Follow the organization's records policy and any contractual requirement. A log that keeps everything forever creates risk without adding decision value. The retention rule should name who approves an exception."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Review the log on a schedule tied to the partnership cycle. A quarterly review is enough for stable sources, while an active negotiation may need a weekly check. Flag entries that have passed their next review date and assign an owner. The review should confirm the claim, update the access date, and note any change in confidence. The review should add one new entry rather than rewrite the old one.","When a source changes, do not overwrite the old entry. Add the new source, date it, and mark the previous entry as superseded. That history shows how the team's understanding evolved and protects the decision if it is questioned later. It also gives the researcher a clear pattern for handling the next change. The superseded entry should keep its original access date."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A source log cannot prove that a partnership will succeed. It can only show what the team knew and when. The log may miss informal context that shaped the decision, and the confidence labels depend on the reviewer's judgment. The team should state those limits when the log is used in a formal review. The limits should be written on the first page of the log.","Do not treat the presence of a link as proof of quality. A source can be current, relevant, and still wrong. The reviewer should check the source itself when the claim is material. The log is a map to the evidence, not a substitute for reading it. A material claim deserves a direct read, not only a glance at the log."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the number of active entries, the number past review, and the distribution by confidence. Add a short note on the sources that changed since the last review. These counts show whether the log is maintained and where the evidence is weak. They are more useful than a simple count of links. A simple table by partner and confidence makes the gaps easy to see.","Use the log in partnership briefs, renewal decisions, and handoffs. Link to the specific entry rather than the whole document. When a claim is challenged, the team can open the entry and see the source, the date, and the confidence. That is the practical test of whether the log is doing its job. A log that is never opened during a decision is not yet part of the workflow."]},{"heading":"Keeping the log alive between reviews","paragraphs":["A source log fails when it is treated as a one-time collection rather than a maintained record. Give one person the job of checking the review queue each week and assigning expired entries to the researcher who owns the partner. Keep the check short: confirm the link, update the access date, and note any change in confidence. The weekly rhythm prevents a large cleanup before an important meeting.","When a partner relationship ends, archive the log rather than deleting it. The evidence may still matter for a renewal, a dispute, or a future reference check. Remove personal details that are no longer needed and keep the source citations that support the decision. An archived log with clear dates is more useful than a deleted folder that no one can reconstruct."]}],"sources":[{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Census Business Builder","url":"https://www.census.gov/data/data-tools/cbb.html"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}]},{"slug":"can-a-crm-stage-change-be-traced-to-its-supporting-evidence","title":"Can a CRM stage change be traced to its supporting evidence?","summary":"A traceability test for stage changes, with a reconstruction method, evidence chain fields, and a clear statement of limits.","cluster":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"A stage change is traceable when the record shows the evidence, the approver, the timestamp, and the specific criterion that the change satisfied.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/can-a-crm-stage-change-be-traced-to-its-supporting-evidence.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks whether a CRM stage change can be traced back to the evidence that justified it. The unit is one stage transition on one opportunity. The test is whether a reviewer can reconstruct the reason from the record without asking the person who made the change. The scope covers stage changes, not forecast accuracy or the quality of the underlying opportunity. The review should state which stage definitions were in force during the sample.","The question matters because stages drive forecasts, handoffs, and management attention. A stage that moves without evidence can create a commitment the team cannot support. A stage that stays put despite strong evidence can hide a real opportunity. Traceability makes both errors visible and gives the team a way to correct them. A stage that cannot be defended makes the forecast harder to trust."]},{"heading":"Why traceability is difficult","paragraphs":["CRM stages are often changed in a hurry at the end of a call or a week. The person making the change knows the context, so the record gets a short note or no note at all. Later, the context is gone and the stage looks like an opinion. The team then debates the stage instead of the evidence. The record should show the context that the stage label alone cannot carry.","A second problem is that one stage can mean different things to different people. Qualified may mean a fit check, a discovery call, or a budget conversation depending on who is asked. Without a written definition and a required evidence field, the same label can describe very different situations. Traceability starts with a shared definition. A shared definition is the first requirement for a traceable change."]},{"heading":"The evidence chain","paragraphs":["A traceable change needs four links: the criterion, the evidence, the approver, and the timestamp. The criterion says which stage definition was met. The evidence shows the source or conversation that supports it. The approver is the person accountable for the change. The timestamp records when the decision was made. Each link should be visible in the opportunity record without a separate export.","The chain should live in the opportunity record, not in a separate document. If the CRM cannot store the full evidence, store a link and a short summary in a structured field. The reviewer should not have to search a chat archive to find the missing link. A chain that is hard to find is not a usable chain. The sample should include at least one transition that was later reversed."]},{"heading":"Unit and sample","paragraphs":["Define the unit as a single transition, including the stage before and the stage after. Sample transitions across stages, owners, and account types. Include changes that were later reversed, because reversals often reveal a weak criterion. Exclude bulk imports that did not involve a human decision. The period should be stated so the reader can compare it with other reviews.","Choose the sample size and date range before reviewing. Record the selection rule so the review can be repeated. If a stage definition changes during the period, separate the transitions before and after the change. Mixing them makes the result hard to interpret. A stage definition change should split the sample into before and after."]},{"heading":"Reconstruction test","paragraphs":["Ask a reviewer who did not make the change to reconstruct the reason from the record alone. The reviewer should be able to state the criterion, the evidence, and the approver without opening a chat or calling the owner. If the reviewer has to guess, the record is not traceable. The reviewer should record where the record was unclear, not fill the gap from memory.","Use a short form with the four links and a place to mark what is missing. Do not let the original owner explain the change during the test. After the test, compare the reviewer's reconstruction with the owner's account. The differences show which link is weakest and where the template or the training needs work. The reconstruction should be done before the owner explains the change."]},{"heading":"Scoring","paragraphs":["Score each link as present and verifiable, present but vague, or missing. Keep the link-level detail rather than collapsing everything into one number. A change can have a timestamp and an approver but no evidence, which is a different problem from a change with evidence but no owner. The detail determines the repair. The three levels should have written examples so two reviewers apply them the same way.","Report the results by stage and by link. If the evidence link is weakest at the proposal stage, the team may need a required attachment before that transition. If the approver link is weakest for small opportunities, the rule may be too heavy for the risk. The score should guide a specific change. The result should be read by stage before it is read by owner."]},{"heading":"Reading the gaps","paragraphs":["A missing evidence link may mean the stage definition is unclear or the field is hard to use. A missing approver may mean the change is allowed by default for some users. A missing timestamp may be a system limitation rather than a human error. Investigate the cause before changing the policy. The fix may be a field change, a training note, or a new approval rule.","Look for patterns in reversals. A stage that is frequently moved forward and back may have a definition that is too broad. A stage that is rarely reversed but often disputed may have a definition that is too narrow. The pattern is more useful than a single bad record. A reversal that happens twice for the same reason is a process signal."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The test measures the record, not the truth of the opportunity. A well-documented change can still be a bad decision. The reviewer may interpret the criterion differently from the owner, and the sample may miss changes made in bulk or outside the CRM. The team should state these limits when the results are shared. The report should say which stages were not sampled.","Do not use traceability as a reason to add more fields than the team will maintain. A short chain that people actually complete is better than a detailed form that gets skipped. The goal is a record that supports a decision, not a perfect audit trail for every minor update. A short chain that is completed every time is the better design."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the sample size, the link-level results, and the stages with the weakest evidence. Add one or two anonymized examples of a strong chain and a weak one. The examples make the standard concrete and give the team something to copy. Keep the report short enough to act on. The examples should show the missing link, not only the finished record.","Use the findings to change the stage definitions, the required fields, or the approval rule. Start with the stage where the evidence link is weakest. Then repeat the test on a new sample and compare. The goal is a CRM record that lets the team defend a stage change without relying on memory. A follow-up test after the change shows whether the repair worked."]},{"heading":"Making traceability part of the weekly rhythm","paragraphs":["Traceability improves when the check happens close to the change, not months later. Add a short weekly review of the transitions that moved the furthest or affected the largest accounts. Ask the owner to confirm the evidence link while the context is still fresh. A five-minute check is easier than reconstructing a quarter of stage changes before a forecast review.","Use the review to reinforce the stage definitions rather than to catch people. If a transition lacks evidence, ask what the owner knew and where it was recorded. The answer may reveal a missing field or a step that the team does not value. Fix the record and the definition together so the next transition is easier to trace. A short note on the change keeps the lesson visible for the next reviewer."]}],"sources":[{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"}]},{"slug":"what-happens-to-pipeline-coverage-when-one-bdr-is-out-for-a-week","title":"What happens to pipeline coverage when one BDR is out for a week?","summary":"A continuity analysis for shared BDR queues, with coverage definitions, reassignment tests, and a realistic planning view.","cluster":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-06","finding":"Coverage is not the number of people available; it is the share of active follow-ups that can still be prepared, sent, and reviewed within the service window when one person is absent.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-06/what-happens-to-pipeline-coverage-when-one-bdr-is-out-for-a-week.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what happens to pipeline coverage when one BDR is unavailable for a week. The unit is the active follow-up queue owned or shared by that BDR at the start of the absence. The measure is the share of tasks that still receive a timely touch, a review, or a documented reassignment. The scope excludes revenue forecasting and the performance of the absent person. The measure should be defined before the absence, not after the queue is reviewed.","The question matters because coverage is often described as a headcount ratio. A team may believe it has coverage because two people remain, even when neither has the context or the capacity to handle the absent person's queue. The real test is whether the work can continue without hidden delays or dropped commitments. The scope should exclude the performance of the absent person."]},{"heading":"Why coverage is misjudged","paragraphs":["A shared queue hides ownership gaps. Tasks may look assigned because they sit in a team view, while no individual has the context to act. The first sign of trouble is often a missed follow-up that no one noticed until the buyer followed up again. By then, the coverage problem has already affected the relationship. A shared queue can look covered while no individual has the context to act.","Another misjudgment is counting availability without counting skill. A colleague may have time but lack the product knowledge or the relationship history for a specific account. Reassignment is not a simple transfer of a task list. It requires a handoff note, a source link, and enough context to continue the conversation. The backup should confirm that they can open the record and find the next step."]},{"heading":"Definitions","paragraphs":["Define active follow-ups as tasks with a next date inside the absence window or within the service window after it. Exclude closed tasks and tasks explicitly paused by the buyer. Define coverage as the share of active tasks that receive a qualifying action: a send, a call, a review, or a documented reassignment with a new owner and date. The coverage definition should be stated in the report.","Define the service window before the analysis. A team that promises a two-day follow-up has a different coverage problem from a team that promises five days. Use business hours or calendar days consistently. State the definition in the report so the result can be compared across periods. The same window should be used for every task in the sample."]},{"heading":"Data sources","paragraphs":["Use the queue system for task ownership and dates, the CRM for account context, and the outreach tool for send activity. Compare the three sources before the absence and after it. A task that appears open in one system and closed in another is a coverage risk even if the numbers look fine. A task that is open in two systems and closed in one needs a manual check.","Capture the reassignment history, including who received the task and whether they accepted it. A task that was reassigned but never opened is not covered. Add the handoff note status and the account tier. These attributes help explain why some tasks continue and others stall. A reassignment without an explicit acceptance is only a suggestion, not coverage."]},{"heading":"Simulation method","paragraphs":["Build the analysis around a real absence or a planned test. List the active tasks at the start of the window, the owner, the next date, and the context available. Then track what happened to each task during the window. Do not rely on a hypothetical reassignment that no one actually performed. The tabletop should include at least one task with a difficult handoff.","If a real absence is not available, run a tabletop exercise with a small set of tasks. Ask a colleague to pick up the queue using only the written handoff. Record where they had to ask for help. The exercise is not a substitute for a real test, but it exposes the gaps in the record and the assignment rule. The exercise notes should name the missing context, not the person."]},{"heading":"Coverage measures","paragraphs":["Report the share of active tasks with a qualifying action inside the service window. Add the share with a complete handoff note, the share reassigned to a named owner, and the share that missed the window. These four counts separate capacity from record quality and ownership. The count behind each share should be reported so the reader can judge the base.","Also report the age of the oldest untouched task and the number of tasks that changed owner more than once. A small number of very old tasks can signal a hidden backlog. Multiple ownership changes can signal a task that no one wants or understands. Both are useful for the next absence plan. A task that changes owner repeatedly may need a single accountable person."]},{"heading":"Reading the results","paragraphs":["A high action rate with a low handoff rate suggests the team is relying on memory and personal relationships. That may work for a short absence but fails when the person is away longer or leaves. A high handoff rate with a low action rate suggests the reassignment is formal but the capacity is not real. The segment with the most misses should be the first repair target.","Look at the tasks that missed the window. Are they concentrated in one account tier, one product, or one owner? A pattern in a single segment is easier to fix than a queue-wide problem. A pattern across the whole queue may require a different staffing model or a smaller service promise. A queue-wide pattern may call for a smaller service promise or more staff."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A one-week absence is a short window and may not reveal the problems that appear over a month. The analysis depends on accurate task status and may miss informal work that never entered the queue. The team may also change its behavior because it knows the absence is being studied. The analysis should state the dates and the system behind the counts.","Do not use the result to argue that one person is dispensable. The purpose is to find the context and capacity gaps that make coverage fragile. A team with strong coverage still needs the absent person's knowledge when they return. The plan should include a reintegration step, not just a reassignment. The reintegration note should list the tasks that changed owner and their status."]},{"heading":"Planning use","paragraphs":["Use the findings to build a simple absence plan. Name a backup for each active account, require a handoff note before the absence, and set a check-in point during the week. Keep the plan short enough to use. A plan that requires a full audit before every vacation will not be followed. The plan should name the person who confirms each handoff is complete.","Repeat the analysis after the next absence and compare the counts. If coverage improved, identify which change made the difference. If it did not, examine the handoff quality and the service window before adding more people. The goal is a queue that can continue with clear ownership and honest limits. A short review after the absence is more useful than a long retrospective."]},{"heading":"Building the next absence plan","paragraphs":["The next absence plan should be written while the queue is calm, not on the morning someone calls in sick. List the active accounts, the backup owner, the handoff note status, and the next date for each. Keep the list in the same system the team uses for the queue so it does not become another document to maintain. Review it at the start of every month and before any planned leave.","A good plan also names what will not be covered. Not every account needs the same touch during a one-week absence, and pretending otherwise creates false comfort. Decide which accounts can wait, which need a short check-in, and which need a full handoff. That explicit choice is more honest than a coverage ratio that treats every task as equal."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Cybersecurity Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]}];
